  "solution": "The lines are $\\dfrac{x}{6} = \\dfrac{y+2}{6} = \\dfrac{z-1}{1}$ and $\\dfrac{x+1}{12} = \\dfrac{y}{6} = \\dfrac{z}{-1}$.\n\n$\\vec{a}_1 = -2\\hat{j} + \\hat{k}$, $\\vec{b}_1 = 6\\hat{i} + 6\\hat{j} + \\hat{k}$, $\\vec{a}_2 = -\\hat{i}$, $\\vec{b}_2 = 12\\hat{i} + 6\\hat{j} - \\hat{k}$\n\n$$\\vec{b}_1 \\times \\vec{b}_2 = \\begin{vmatrix} \\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 6 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & 6 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = -12\\hat{i} + 18\\hat{j} - 36\\hat{k}$$\n\n$$d = \\frac{|(\\vec{a}_2 - \\vec{a}_1)\\cdot(\\vec{b}_1 \\times \\vec{b}_2)|}{|\\vec{b}_1 \\times \\vec{b}_2|} = \\frac{|(-\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j} - \\hat{k})\\cdot(-12\\hat{i} + 18\\hat{j} - 36\\hat{k})|}{\\sqrt{144 + 324 + 1296}} = \\frac{12 + 36 + 36}{\\sqrt{1764}} = \\frac{84}{42} = 2$$",
  "chapter": "Three Dimensional Geometry",
  "subtopic": "Shortest distance between skew lines",
  "archetype": "formula application",
  "difficulty": 3
 },
